Mitch

Prof Borchers offers the following suggestion.  Place "observer" as a covariate in the mrmodel.  If you don’t explicitly put observer in, you are fitting a model in which the primary and secondary observers’ detection functions are constrained to be the same.

Here’s an example got by modifying some code in the file “mrds-golftees-distill.Rmd” file that is available on the distance sampling site:
http://examples.distancesampling.org/mrds-golftees/mrds-golftees-distill.Rmd  Note the comments included in the code below.  Specifically, note the first analysis produces identical p0 estimates for each observer, the second analysis produces observer-specific estimates.

> # fit IO model without observer
> io.fi.dist <- ddf(method='io.fi <http://io.fi>', mrmodel=~glm(link='logit',formula=~distance), data=detections, meta.data=list(width=4))
>
> # fit IO model WITH observer
> io.fi.dist_obs <- ddf(method='io.fi <http://io.fi>', mrmodel=~glm(link='logit',formula=~distance+observer), data=detections, meta.data=list(width=4))
>
> # Look at output for IO model without observer
> # Note that "Average primary p(0)" and “Average secondary p(0)” estimates are identical
> # -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
