Elaine

You are correct that the reported SE is SE of log(parameter estimate).

To find the SE on the real scale, make use of the delta method approximation.  Consult Powell (2007) if you've not met the delta method before.

The delta method works with variances, rather than SEs.  You'll need to convert your SEs to variances, perform the delta method calculation, then square root to produce SE of the transformed variance.

My math is probably dubious this morning, so best to check, but I think this is a possible answer

new.se <http://new.se> <- sqrt(exp(se)^2 * exp(se)^2)

Powell, Larkin A. 2007. Approximating variance of demographic parameters using the delta method: a reference for avian biologists. The Condor 109 (4): 949–54. MailScanner has detected a possible fraud attempt from "link.getmailspring.com" claiming to be https://doi.org/10.1650/0010-5422(2007)109[949:AVODPU]2.0.CO;2 <https://link.getmailspring.com/link/BA1...@getmailspring.com/0?redirect=https%3A%2F%2Fdoi.org%2F10.1650%2F0010-5422(2007)109%5B949%3AAVODPU%5D2.0.CO%3B2&recipient=ZGlzdGFuY2Utc2FtcGxpbmdAZ29vZ2xlZ3JvdXBzLmNvbQ%3D%3D>.

